1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,570
Have you ever wondered how an industrial robot

2
00:00:03,571 --> 00:00:06,999
knows exactly which way to turn its gripper?

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,930
In robotics, orientation describes which way a tool is facing.

4
00:00:11,931 --> 00:00:14,321
We can rotate it about three

5
00:00:14,322 --> 00:00:16,786
coordinate axes: X, Y, and Z.

6
00:00:16,787 --> 00:00:19,999
Each rotation is described by an angle.

7
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,991
But how do we represent these rotations mathematically?

8
00:00:23,992 --> 00:00:26,901
That’s where rotation matrices come in!

9
00:00:26,902 --> 00:00:29,676
A rotation matrix is a three-by-three

10
00:00:29,677 --> 00:00:32,653
matrix containing sine and cosine terms.

11
00:00:32,654 --> 00:00:34,886
It allows us to calculate how

12
00:00:34,887 --> 00:00:37,999
coordinates change when an object rotates.

13
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,059
For example, when a robotic gripper rotates

14
00:00:41,060 --> 00:00:44,055
ninety degrees about the Z-axis, its local

15
00:00:44,056 --> 00:00:47,832
X-axis points along the original positive Y-direction.

16
00:00:47,833 --> 00:00:51,999
The rotation matrix describes this transformation precisely.

17
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,572
And that’s how industrial robots can calculate tool

18
00:00:54,573 --> 00:00:58,221
orientations for precise assembly, welding, and pick-and-place operations.

19
00:00:58,222 --> 00:00:59,999
Follow for more robotics concepts!

